  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This is a flat scarf worked from one short end to the other in "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "feather-and-fan"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "feather and fan"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". This four-row lace repeat from the Shetland tradition is worked on 4 mm needles with DK yarn, finishing at 20 cm wide and 175 cm long after blocking. On the pattern row, grouped decreases draw the fabric in and yarn-overs fan it back out, forming the wave along the length."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Flat construction was chosen because feather and fan is a traditional flat lace pattern worked in rows. Only one row in four is a pattern row (Row 3); the other three rows are plain knit or purl, so the pattern is straightforward to follow. Blocking is essential: without blocking, the waves look contracted and the scarf is several centimetres shorter and narrower than its finished dimensions. The scarf uses about 175 g (395 m / 432 yards) of DK yarn."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "suppliesCard",
        "attrs": {
          "heading": "Materials",
          "items": [
            {
              "name": "DK-weight wool or wool-blend yarn",
              "qty": "175 g (approx. 395 m / 432 yards)",
              "substitutions": "Wool or wool-blend blocks beautifully. A smooth 4-ply or DK in a solid or semi-solid colour shows the wave pattern clearly."
            },
            {
              "name": "4 mm needles (UK 8 / US 6)",
              "qty": "1 pair or 1 circular, 40 cm or longer",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Blocking mat and rust-proof pins",
              "qty": "required for lace",
              "substitutions": "Foam tiles and T-pins work well."
            },
            {
              "name": "Tapestry or darning needle",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Scissors",
              "qty": "1 pair",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Measuring tape",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Row counter",
              "qty": "optional",
              "substitutions": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gauge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "22 sts x 30 rows = 10 x 10 cm in stocking stitch on 4 mm needles, blocked. Feather and fan opens to approximately 18 sts per 10 cm after blocking. Blocking changes the dimensions significantly: a scarf that measures 15 cm wide off the needles can measure 20 cm after a thorough wet block. Each stitch off gauge changes the finished width by about 1.1 cm after blocking."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitches used"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "K (knit), P (purl), "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "yarn-over"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "YO"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " (yarn over), "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "k2tog"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "K2tog"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " (right-leaning decrease), "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "ssk"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "SSK"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " (left-leaning decrease). The decreases and yarn-overs balance so the stitch count stays constant throughout."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pattern"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast on"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Using the long-tail cast-on, cast on 38 stitches: 1 selvedge stitch each side and two full 18-stitch repeats of the feather and fan pattern. Tail length for 4 mm needles (circumference approximately 12.6 mm) and 38 stitches: (12.6 x 38) / 10 + 15 = about 63 cm."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitch count check-in: 38 stitches after the cast-on."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Body"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 1 (RS): K to end. (38 sts)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 2 (WS): P to end. (38 sts)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 3 (RS pattern row): K1 (selvedge), *(K2tog) 3 times, (YO, K1) 6 times, (SSK) 3 times; repeat from * once more, K1 (selvedge). (38 sts: the 6 decreases and 6 YOs in each repeat cancel out.)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 4 (WS): P to end. (38 sts)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Repeat Rows 1-4 until the scarf measures 175 cm from the cast-on edge, blocked. Off the needles it will be shorter; allow for the blocking expansion. A useful approach is to work until the piece measures about 155 cm off the needles, then block a small section to check the blocking ratio before deciding whether to continue. At the halfway mark (about 87 cm), count: still 38 stitches."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast off"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast off after a Row 1 or Row 2 using the standard method. Work loosely; a tight cast-off on lace resists blocking."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Common faults"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitch count changing on the pattern row. The most common error is forgetting one of the six yarn-overs or one of the six decreases in a repeat, so the counts don't balance. After every Row 3, count the stitches: still 38. If not, check whether the YOs and K2togs/SSKs in each repeat are equal in number."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Wave pattern not visible. The waves appear clearly only after blocking. Off the needles the fabric looks compressed. Do not judge the pattern before the scarf is wet-blocked."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Lace pattern breaking up. A dropped stitch in the pattern row can unravel differently from plain knitting because adjacent yarn-overs are nearby. Thread a lifeline (a smooth contrasting yarn threaded through all live stitches) every 50 rows or so to limit the damage if you need to rip back."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finishing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weave in ends. Wet "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "blocking"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "blocking"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is mandatory for lace: soak the scarf in cool water for 20 minutes until fully saturated. Press out moisture without wringing. Pin flat on a blocking mat, stretching the scarf to its full 20 cm width and 175 cm length. Pin the scalloped cast-on edge and cast-off edge into their wave shapes. Leave to dry overnight."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Care"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Handwash in cool water with wool wash. Lay flat to dry each time. Lace does not need re-pinning after the first blocking unless the shape is distorted."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What to try next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The fern lace scarf uses a more complex pattern row, and the lace stole uses a similar wave structure in fingering-weight yarn for a finer, larger piece."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}